.TH KAGI-CONFIG 1 "November 2026" "Kagi 0.39.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kagi-config \- model Kagi failure domains, optimize chunk geometry, and emit a 60-bit placement plan
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kagi-config
.RB { \-c " " \fIFILE\fR " | " \-\-wizard " | " \-\-resume " " \fIDIR\fR }
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs Monte Carlo and deterministic analysis of concurrent site, rack, host, network and disk failures. It compares Reed-Solomon, LRC, MSR and CLAY chunk geometries and emits a placement salt, equal host ranges and validated deployment policies.
.PP
The minimum supported topology is six servers across at least two racks, with at least six disks per server. A policy must tolerate concurrent disk, host and rack failures. Site and network tolerance apply where those domains have multiple members. With exactly two sites, a policy may explicitly allow zero site failures after the operator types I ACCEPT THAT LOSING A SITE CAN MAKE OBJECTS UNAVAILABLE.
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Read topology and policy inputs from YAML.
.TP
.B \-\-wizard
Build the input interactively.
Quick mode asks for counts of sites, racks per site, hosts per rack, and NVMe
disks per host. It uses 8 TB NVMe disks, default failure and repair rates,
automatic EC geometry, and two modeled network paths per host on distinct
sequential /24 subnets from 10.0.0.0/8.
The full wizard asks for disk classes, capacities, failure assumptions,
multi-homing, and policy constraints.
.TP
.BI \-\-wizard-config-output " FILE"
Save the wizard's reusable input.
The default is
.IR kagi.wizard.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-mount-root " DIR"
Override the root for generated disk mount paths.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the generated plan as YAML.
The default is
.IR kagi.generated.yaml .
The same plan is printed to standard output. It includes equal host ranges
and per-policy deployments that can be loaded through planner_output.
A finite simulation with zero observed losses leaves the estimate unresolved.
.TP
.BI \-\-join-key-output " FILE"
Write the base64 cluster admission key.
The default is
.IR kagi.join.key .
Restrict access to this file and to any checkpoint containing the key.
.SH SIMULATION OPTIONS
The defaults below apply to a full run.
.TP
.BI \-\-trials " N"
Ordinary Monte Carlo trials per placement candidate.
The default is 100000.
.TP
.BI \-\-candidates " N"
Placement salts to evaluate.
The default is 256.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " N"
Seed for the simulation.
The default is 12648430.
.TP
.BI \-\-rare-trials " N"
Importance-sampling trials per finalist.
The default is 100000.
.TP
.BI \-\-finalists " N"
Number of ordinary Monte Carlo finalists to reevaluate.
The default is 8.
.TP
.B --validation-keys N
Accepted for compatibility. Validation covers every distinct host-range layout.
.TP
.BI \-\-time-trials " N"
Continuous-time trajectories.
The default is 20000.
.TP
.BI \-\-horizon-years " YEARS"
Years per continuous-time trajectory.
The default is 10.
.TP
.B --max-storage-overhead RATIO
Maximum nominal stored fragment payload ratio for a candidate geometry.
.TP
.B --example-object-size-bytes BYTES
Object size used for storage overhead comparisons. Repeat to compare several sizes.
.TP
.B --chunk-replicas N
Store N physical copies of each encoded chunk.
.TP
.B --object-replicas N
Force N full-object copies instead of erasure coding.
.TP
.BI \-\-ais-rounds " N"
Adaptive importance-sampling rounds.
The default is 4.
.TP
.BI \-\-elite-fraction " FRACTION"
Near-miss fraction used to adapt rare-event sampling.
The default is 0.10.
.TP
.BI \-\-run-mode " full|quick30|quick60"
Select the simulation workload.
Quick modes replace the corresponding full-run sampling and trajectory
settings with smaller presets.
They trade statistical confidence for shorter runs.
The numbers in their names indicate approximate runtime classes.
.TP
.BI \-\-mc-backend " auto|cpu|cuda|hip|opencl"
Select the backend for batched erasure-code readability scoring.
The default is
.BR auto .
GPU choices require the corresponding build feature and runtime.
Topology and failure generation and deterministic validation run on the CPU.
.SH CHECKPOINTS
.TP
.BI \-\-checkpoint " DIR"
Save normalized inputs, run options, the admission key, and completed
simulation batches.
.TP
.BI \-\-checkpoint-seconds " N"
Checkpoint interval checked at batch boundaries.
The default is 60 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-\-checkpoint-batch-trials " N"
Trials per saved batch.
The default is 1024.
Batch size controls the reduction order and the granularity of unsaved work.
.TP
.BI \-\-resume " DIR"
Resume using the saved inputs and options.
The checkpoint records the Kagi version and absolute output paths.
Use the same Kagi version when resuming.
The resume form rejects conflicting simulation, input, and output options.
.SH PROGRESS
The progress panel reports phase progress, simulations completed, simulation
rate, and the lowest-loss placement and geometries evaluated so far.
It appears only on an interactive standard error stream.
.TP
.B \-\-no-progress
Disable the progress panel.
.TP
.BI \-\-progress-interval-ms " N"
Set the panel refresh interval.
The default is 150 milliseconds.
The display clamps this interval to 50 through 2000 milliseconds.
.SH COMMAND INFORMATION
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the Kagi version.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
kagi-config \-\-config examples/example.yaml \-\-run-mode quick30
kagi-config \-\-wizard \-\-checkpoint planner-run
kagi-config \-\-resume planner-run \-\-no-progress
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kagi-cluster-host (1),
.BR kagi-host (1),
.BR kagi-run (1)
